There's a solution to this that seems obvious to me, but maybe it isn't.
Once you've found an altar, sacrifice until it is safe to pray. If
you have to pray, return to the altar, use a wand of create monster,
sacrifice until it is safe to pray. This way, before you approach new
situations where you might need to #pray, you will always know that doing
so is safe.  This requires knowing what things will reset your prayer
timeout (wishing is the one that has tripped me up in the past, but the
number of turns there is pretty safely bounded if I remember correctly),
which is a matter of research.  It also requires backtracking, which is
boring, but optimal safe strategy is often boring. 

This doesn't work in the very early game (might not have an altar, might
not have a wand of create monster, might otherwise be constrained by the
food clock) but once you've finished Sokoban or so this should work all
the time. I consider this one of the many little things that add up to
"NetHack is only challenging in the early game." Prayer is a renewable
resource where time on the food clock (inexpensive post-Soko for most
players) and time on the wall clock can be exchanged for a Get Out Of
Jail Free card.

When I play NetHack, I don't usually bother keeping strict track of
this anymore, because I don't generally need it, and because I have
an instinctive sense of whether or not it is safe to pray. But if you
do find yourself needing prayer, and you have an altar and a couple of
food rations, you can guarantee it, assuming you don't get ambushed by
another prayer-requiring situation on your way back to the altar. That
should be preventable by carrying multiple escape mechanisms with you,
at least 99.99 percent of the time.

